From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Anonymous Submission #60 <anon@example.invalid>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 12:56:41 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] RNGuard: isolate the kernel RNG's state behind a hardware
 gate

RNGuard places the kernel RNG's long-lived state -- the base ChaCha20 key
(base_crng), the per-CPU generators (crngs) and the four batched-entropy
pools -- in a dedicated .rnguard_iso section, and wraps every RNG code path
that touches it in an enable/disable gate pair. Outside a gate window the
memory is unreadable and unwritable, so an attacker holding an arbitrary
kernel read-write primitive cannot disclose or corrupt the generator state.

Transient state is covered too. The RNG's working values -- the ChaCha20
state, the fast-key-erasure first block, the reseed key -- live on the kernel
stack, where an attacker can race them even while the persistent state is
sealed. RNGuard therefore switches the stack pointer to a protected per-CPU
pseudo-stack for the duration of each gate window, which covers compiler
spills as well as named locals. Interrupts are disabled across the window
because exception entry would otherwise push a hardware frame onto the
protected stack.

This patch is the architecture-independent half: the Kconfig options, the
.rnguard_iso placement macros, and the gate and stack-switch call sites in
drivers/char/random.c. It is inert on its own -- one of the two backend
patches that follow supplies the actual hardware mechanism.

With both CONFIG_RNGUARD_* options off, the preprocessed source of
random.c is upstream's, unchanged: the conditionals are at the call sites
rather than hidden behind macros that expand to the upstream call.
